            <ArticleTitle>Long-term prognosis of patients with acute coronary syndrome and moderate coronary artery stenosis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>90-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We evaluated the clinical outcome and the prognostic factors at 6-year follow-up of patients with acute coronary syndrome without critical coronary arterial narrowing. The mean follow-up was 73 +/- 19 months. Mortality rate was 13%, and 20 patients (12%) had major cardiac event, 8 patients (5%) had stroke and 10 patients (6%) underwent revascularization. Multivariate analysis matched for age and ejection factor showed that moderate disease (stenosis 40-59%) (OR = 2.713, p &lt; 0.024) was an independent predictive factor of major cardiac event.</AbstractText>
